input.minimal_fix:
File: /output/test2.rs

Add an extern declaration above `fn main`, and restore SIG_DFL as the first statement of `main` (it must precede any `println!`).

Replace:

    use crate::indicators::{Percentage, ProgressBar};

    fn main() {
        // `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` accepts arguments but never reads
        // them, so the output is the same for any command line.
        let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();

with:

    use crate::indicators::{Percentage, ProgressBar};

    extern "C" {
        fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize;
    }

    fn main() {
        // The Rust runtime sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN before main; the C++ binary
        // leaves it at SIG_DFL. Restore the C default so that a closed stdout
        // kills us by signal 13 (wait status 141, silent) instead of panicking
        // inside the stdout LineWriter (status 101 + 193 bytes on stderr).
        unsafe { signal(13 /* SIGPIPE */, 0 /* SIG_DFL */) };

        // `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` accepts arguments but never reads
        // them, so the output is the same for any command line.
        let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();

Nothing else changes; no files under /output/src need touching, and no dependency is added (std-only, edition 2021, rustc 1.75 compatible). Verified above: builds clean under both `rustc -O test2.rs` and `cargo build --release` with 0 warnings, stdout stays byte-identical to the reference, and closed-stdout yields status 141 with empty stderr in both the deterministic harness and 30/30 `| true` repeats.

Do NOT expect this to also fix the /dev/full write-failure divergence (C++ exits 0 silently, Rust prints a 205-byte panic). That requires a separate change — replacing `println!` with an explicit `write!`/`flush` whose Err is discarded, mirroring libstdc++ setting badbit and ignoring it — and should be tracked as its own finding.
